Easier: use eval to compute screen projection of HQ position using __G and camera — I can project in page JS: create vector from building pos +1 y, project with camera, convert to pixels using renderer domElement rect. Then call the canvas event handlers directly by dispatching PointerEvents? The handlers use 'pointerdown'/'pointerup' listeners on canvas/window — I can dispatch synthetic PointerEvent with clientX/Y. That tests the real pipeline.

Let me do that:

```js
() => {
  const G = window.__G;
  const cam = G.camera;
  const b = G.buildings.find(b=>b.side==='player'&&b.type==='hq');
  const v = b.group.position.clone(); v.y += 2;
  v.project(cam);
  const rect = G.renderer.domElement.getBoundingClientRect();
  const x = (v.x*0.5+0.5)*rect.width + rect.left;
  const y = (-v.y*0.5+0.5)*rect.height + rect.top;
  const ev = (type) => new PointerEvent(type, {clientX:x, clientY:y, button:0, pointerId:1, bubbles:true});
  G.renderer.domElement.dispatchEvent(ev('pointerdown'));
  window.dispatchEvent(ev('pointerup'));
  return {x,y, selection: G.selection.map(e=>e.type)};
}
```

